 title:'Tempo and time signature', short:'Set the speed of the pulse and choose how many beats go in a bar.',
 time:15, chapter:0, module:'Time', tag:'BPM & meter', lab:'beat',
 opens:'By the end you can read 4/4, 3/4 and 6/8, convert a tempo into seconds, and pick a tempo that suits a song.',
 blocks:[
  {h:'Tempo is beats per minute',
   p:['<b>Tempo</b> is how fast the pulse runs, measured in <b>beats per minute</b> — BPM. At 60 BPM a beat arrives every second, which is roughly a resting heartbeat. At 120 BPM they arrive twice a second.',
      'The arithmetic is one division and you will use it constantly: one beat lasts 60 ÷ BPM seconds. At 96 BPM that is 60 ÷ 96 = 0.625 seconds, so a four-beat bar takes 2.5 seconds and sixteen bars take 40 seconds. That is how you know a song is going to be about forty seconds long before you have recorded any of it.'],
   widget:{kind:'tempo',tempo:96,beats:4,meters:[2,3,4,5],
    title:'Tempo and metre, with the numbers',
    hint:'Drag the tempo and watch the arithmetic update. Then change the metre and count along: the accent always marks beat 1.',
    say:''}},
  {h:'Most music lives between 70 and 140',
   p:['Tempo is a creative choice, not a technical one, but the conventions are strong. Ballads sit around 60–80 BPM, hip-hop around 80–95, most pop and rock around 100–130, house music at 120–128, drum and bass near 174.',
      'Pick a tempo early and leave it alone, because almost everything else you do depends on it. If a song feels wrong and you cannot say why, try it five BPM slower before you change anything else.'],
   widget:{kind:'steps',beats:4,div:2,tempo:96,min:60,max:170,
    title:'One pattern, many tempos',
    hint:'The same backbeat from 60 BPM to 170. Nothing about the pattern changes — but at the extremes it stops being the same kind of music.',
    lanes:[{name:'Hi-hat',id:'hat',on:[1,3,5,7],level:.5},{name:'Snare',id:'snare',on:[2,6]},{name:'Kick',id:'kick',on:[0,4]}],
    say:'A single pattern can be a ballad or a dance track depending only on where that slider sits.'}},
  {h:'The time signature is two numbers',
   p:['A <b>time signature</b> is written as one number above another, like 4/4. The <i>top</i> number is how many beats are in a bar. The <i>bottom</i> number says which note length gets one beat: 4 means a quarter note, 8 means an eighth note.',
      'So 4/4 is four quarter-note beats to a bar — so common it is called <b>common time</b>. 3/4 is three quarter notes, which is a waltz. 2/4 is a march. 6/8 is six eighth notes a bar, which in practice you feel as two groups of three, giving it a rolling, lilting motion.']},
  {h:'Three feels different from four',
   p:['The difference is not academic. In 4/4 you can divide the bar in half and everything stays even. In 3/4 you cannot, so the bar always has an odd lean to it — there is a reason waltzes feel like turning rather than walking.'],
   fig:{kind:'grid',beats:3,div:2,cw:46,laneWidth:96,
    lanes:[{name:'Hi-hat',tone:'blue',on:[0,1,2,3,4,5]},{name:'Snare',tone:'orange',on:[2,4]},{name:'Kick',tone:'green',on:[0]}],
    counts:['1','&','2','&','3','&'],
    alt:'One bar of three-four time. Kick on beat 1, snare on beats 2 and 3, hi-hat on all six half beats.',
    caption:'A bar of <b>3/4</b>. One kick on the downbeat and the snare on the two weak beats. Count “one two three” round and round; there is no halfway point in the bar for a backbeat to sit on, which is why waltzes have none.'},
   widget:{kind:'steps',beats:3,div:2,tempo:104,min:60,max:150,
    title:'Play in three',
    hint:'Six cells instead of eight. Count aloud “one two three” while it loops until the downbeat stops surprising you.',
    lanes:[{name:'Hi-hat',id:'hat',on:[0,1,2,3,4,5],level:.45},{name:'Snare',id:'snare',on:[2,4]},{name:'Kick',id:'kick',on:[0]}],
    say:'Keep counting aloud. In three, losing beat 1 is much easier than in four.'}}
 ],
