 title:'The twelve notes', short:'Find any note on a keyboard using the black keys as landmarks.',
 time:15, chapter:0, module:'Pitch', tag:'Finding C', lab:'keys',
 opens:'By the end you can point at any key and name it, and find every C on any keyboard in a second.',
 blocks:[
  {h:'A keyboard is twelve keys, repeating',
   p:['A piano looks like a wall of keys, which makes it seem harder than it is. In fact there are only twelve different keys, and then the whole pattern starts again. Everything to the right is higher; everything to the left is lower.',
      'When you move twelve keys up — counting black keys as well as white — you arrive at a note that sounds like the one you started on, just higher. That distance is an <b>octave</b>, and the two notes share a name. This is not a convention someone chose; it is what the physics of a vibrating string does, and every musical tradition on earth has noticed it.'],
   fig:{kind:'keys',low:48,high:72,kw:30,groups:true,labels:'c',
    mark:{48:{tone:'orange',text:'C'},60:{tone:'orange',text:'C'},72:{tone:'orange',text:'C'}},
    alt:'Two octaves of a keyboard with every C marked in orange, and the black keys grouped in twos and threes.',
    caption:'Two octaves. The three marked keys are all called <b>C</b> and they sound like the same note at different heights. The bars above the black keys show the landmark pattern: a group of <b>two</b>, then a group of <b>three</b>, forever.'}},
  {h:'The black keys tell you where you are',
   p:['The black keys are not scattered. They come in a group of two, then a group of three, then two, then three. That irregular pattern is the most useful thing on the instrument, because it is how you know which white key you are looking at without counting from the end.',
      'The rule to memorise is this: <b>C is the white key immediately to the left of a group of two black keys.</b> Learn that one landmark and the rest follows, because the white keys from there go C D E F G A B and then back to C.'],
   widget:{kind:'keys',mode:'name',low:48,high:72,labels:'c',
    title:'Press any key and have it named',
    hint:'Find a group of two black keys, then press the white key just left of it. You should get a C every time.',
    say:'Press a few keys at random and guess the name before you look at what it says.'}},
  {h:'Why the pattern is uneven',
   p:['A reasonable question: why not twelve evenly spaced white keys? Because the white keys are not an arbitrary selection — they are the notes of C major, the scale you will meet in two lessons’ time. The black keys are the five notes left over.',
      'That is also why the gaps are uneven. Between most white-key neighbours there is a black key, but between E and F, and between B and C, there is none. Those two pairs sit directly next to each other, and that detail is responsible for a great deal of what follows in this chapter.']},
  {h:'Sharps, flats, and two names for one key',
   p:['The seven letters A to G cover the white keys. The black keys are named by their relationship to a neighbour: a <b>sharp</b> (♯) means one key higher, a <b>flat</b> (♭) means one key lower.',
      'So the black key between C and D is both C♯ (one above C) and D♭ (one below D). Same key, same sound, two names — these are called <b>enharmonic</b> spellings. Which name gets used depends on the key the music is in, which is a lesson 7 problem. For now, sharps are fine for everything.'],
   fig:{kind:'keys',low:60,high:72,kw:38,labels:'all',
    mark:{61:{tone:'slate',text:'C♯'},63:{tone:'slate',text:'D♯'},66:{tone:'slate',text:'F♯'},68:{tone:'slate',text:'G♯'},70:{tone:'slate',text:'A♯'}},
    alt:'One octave from C to C with all seven white keys lettered and the five black keys labelled as sharps.',
    caption:'One octave, fully labelled. Seven white keys named by letter, five black keys named as <b>sharps</b> of the key below them. Each black key also has a flat name: C♯ is D♭, D♯ is E♭, and so on. Notice there is no black key between E and F, or between B and C.'}},
  {h:'Octave numbers pin a note down',
   p:['“C” names twelve or so keys on a piano. To identify one exactly, a number is added: C4 is the C in the middle of the keyboard, usually called <b>middle C</b>, and the C an octave above it is C5. The number changes at every C, not at every A, which surprises people once and then never again.',
      'This course writes pitches that way throughout: C4 is middle C, E4 is four keys above it, C5 is an octave up. When you see C4 in a chord or a melody, it is a single specific key.']}
 ],
 tableLead:'The twelve notes in order, starting from C:',
 cols:['Step','Name','Also written','Key colour'],
 rows:[['1','C','—','White'],['2','C♯','D♭','Black'],['3','D','—','White'],['4','D♯','E♭','Black'],['5','E','—','White'],['6','F','—','White'],['7','F♯','G♭','Black'],['8','G','—','White'],['9','G♯','A♭','Black'],['10','A','—','White'],['11','A♯','B♭','Black'],['12','B','—','White']],
 recap:['There are twelve notes and then the pattern repeats; twelve keys up is an octave and shares its name.','C is the white key just left of a group of two black keys.','Black keys are sharps or flats of their neighbours, so each has two names.','A number after the letter fixes the octave: C4 is middle C.'],
